import * as React from 'react';

/**
 * Accessories for the AgentAvatar's fixed minifig head — the full designer
 * sheet (47 accessories), implemented 1:1 from the reference asset.
 *
 * GENERATED — do not hand-edit. Every accessory was vectorised from the
 * designer's sheet: each cell was upscaled, colour-quantised to its dominant
 * palette, the head located by silhouette matching (position, scale and — for
 * cells where the designer drew a taller head — height), the head and face
 * masked out, the remaining pieces traced with vtracer and refit as cubic
 * Béziers, then expressed in head units so they land on our head exactly as
 * they sit on the sheet's.
 *
 * Geometry lives in a 0–100 viewBox mapped onto the avatar box (the head
 * silhouette is x 0–100 / y 0–100 with the stud at x 26.6–73.4 / y 0–17.7,
 * eyes at (31, 55.5) / (69, 55.5)) with overflow visible so headgear can rise
 * above the box — like the badge, accessories are unclipped.
 *
 * Colours are hardcoded, sampled from the sheet — deliberately not theme
 * tokens, so a piece looks identical on every theme and avatar colour, like
 * moulded plastic. White pieces carry the sheet's hairline grey outline so
 * they read against a light background.
 *
 * Two anchor layers (see BotAvatarProps):
 * - face accessories (eyewear, disguises) ride the face motion — they follow
 *   gaze and drift like something worn on the face;
 * - body accessories (hair and headgear) sit between the silhouette and the
 *   face — they breathe with the body and stay put when the eyes move. The
 *   eyes and mouth render above them, so pieces that frame the face
 *   (goggles, balaclava, beard) keep the face visible through their openings.
 *   An accessory may use both layers (the pirate hat is a body-layer hat plus
 *   a face-layer eyepatch; grandpa is a body-layer visor plus face-layer
 *   glasses).
 */

